  "id": "Compound::DB00495 - Gene::5243",
  "verbalization": "Zidovudine, Lamivudine, and their derivatives are all classified as nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs), a class of antiretroviral drugs. The evidence suggests that these compounds bind to Abcb1, a transporter protein involved in drug efflux and cellular homeostasis. This binding is supported by multiple paths that link Zidovudine and Lamivudine to Abcb1 through a common ancestor in the N-Glycosyl Compound category, which is also part of the NRTI class. The consistent presence of a binding interaction between these compounds and Abcb1, across different paths, indicates a potential mechanism by which Zidovudine and its analogs may influence Abcb1 function. The evidence is further strengthened by the observed causal relationships between Zidovudine and other conditions, such as Oedema Auricular and Mucosal Pigmentation, which are both linked to Lamivudine and Abcb1. While the exact mechanism of Abcb1 interaction remains to be fully elucidated, the shared pathway and binding evidence suggest a plausible role for Zidovudine in modulating Abcb1 activity.",
